Thanks for the reply, @silviuaavram.

I had proposed modding (wrapping) the `aria-valuenow` prop - that should address the accessibility issue (a visually impaired user would get the progress bar as 70% instead of 570 out of 100) while still offering a supported way of representing a cyclical, circular, or ring buffer value.

Perhaps something like this:

```js
// https://stackoverflow.com/a/707589/25507
function wrapToRange(value, min, max) {
  const range = max - min + 1;
  value = ((value - min) % range);
  if (value < 0) {
    return max + 1 + value;
  } else {
    return min + value;
  }
}

  // ...
  if (variant === 'determinate') {
    const circumference = 2 * Math.PI * ((SIZE - thickness) / 2);

    const range = max - min;
    const ariaValue = cyclical ? (value - min) % range + min : value;

    if (process.env.NODE_ENV !== 'production') {
      if (ariaValue < min || ariaValue > max || min >= max) {
        warnOnce(
          `MUI: The min, max, and value props in CircularProgress should be numbers where min < max and (unless cyclical) min <= value <= max. Received min=${min}, max=${max}, value=${value}.`,
        );
      }
    }

    circleStyle.strokeDasharray = circumference.toFixed(3);
    circleStyle.strokeDashoffset =
      range > 0
        ? `${(((max - value) / range) * circumference).toFixed(3)}px`
        : `${circumference.toFixed(3)}px`; // empty-state fallback when range is invalid
    rootStyle.transform = 'rotate(-90deg)';

    rootProps['aria-valuenow'] = ariaValue;
    rootProps['aria-valuemin'] = min;
    rootProps['aria-valuemax'] = max;
  }
```

If you don't want to do that, then we'll make do with a one-time warning, as you're planning on doing.

