### Related page

https://mui.com/material-ui/customization/typography/

### Kind of issue

Missing information

### Issue description

The section about [self-hosted fonts](https://mui.com/material-ui/customization/typography/#self-hosted-fonts) mentions how it is possible to do this with overriding the styles of `MuiCssBaseline`. The documentation lacks mentioning how it can be archived with the `MuiScopedCssBaseline`. I expected it to be the same, but it is different.

With `MuiCssBaseline` the override looks like this:

```
  components: {
    MuiCssBaseline: {
      styleOverrides: `
        @font-face {
          font-family: 'Raleway';
          font-style: normal;
          font-display: swap;
          font-weight: 400;
          src: local('Raleway'), local('Raleway-Regular'), url(${RalewayWoff2}) format('woff2');
        }
      `,
    },
  },
```


With `MuiScopedCssBaseline` the override looks like this:
```
  components: {
    MuiScopedCssBaseline: {
      styleOverrides: {
        root: `
          @font-face {
            font-family: 'Raleway';
            font-style: normal;
            font-display: swap;
            font-weight: 400;
            src: local('Raleway'), local('Raleway-Regular'), url(${RalewayWoff2}) format('woff2');
          }
        `
      },
    },
  },
```


### Context

I wanna make it easier to find why the same values for `styleOverrides` are not working when you are switching from `MuiCssBaseline` to `MuiScopedCssBaseline`.

**Search keywords**: custom font scoped css baseline

### Steps to reproduce

Steps:
1. Open this link to live example: https://stackblitz.com/edit/dlmtqo3v?file=src%2FDemo.tsx
2. Open dev tools
3. Inspect the second `<style data-emotion="css">` tag.

### Current behavior

The CSS contains the following:

```css
.css-1suf81v-MuiListItemText-root .MuiTypography-root:where(&  .MuiListItemText-primary),
.css-1suf81v-MuiListItemText-root .MuiTypography-root:where(&  .MuiListItemText-secondary) {
  display:block;
}
```

`&` here is invalid - it's interpreted as a CSS nested selector, scoped to `document`, not an Emotion / Stylis nested selector, as intended.

### Expected behavior

Correct nesting. Compare with 7.3.8:

```css
.MuiTypography-root:where(.css-cfq8qh-MuiListItemText-root .MuiListItemText-primary) { 
  display:block;
}
.MuiTypography-root:where(.css-cfq8qh-MuiListItemText-root .MuiListItemText-secondary) {
  display:block;
}
```

### Context

I discovered this while comparing Jest snapshots. It appears to be the result of a bug in @emotion/cache (see https://github.com/emotion-js/emotion/issues/3366) and was triggered by the refactoring in #47838.

