### Steps to reproduce

Steps:
1. Open this link to live example: https://stackblitz.com/edit/github-3wcrfxkj?file=src%2FApp.tsx
2. In the "Original button" section, observe that the `startIcon` row's button text sits higher than the label text — the baselines don't align
3. Compare with the `endIcon` and no-icon rows, which align correctly
4. In the "Button with `::before`" section, observe that all three rows align correctly

### Current behavior

When a Button with startIcon is inside a flex/grid container using `align-items: baseline`, the button's text baseline does not align with sibling text elements. Buttons with endIcon or no icon align correctly.

The `.MuiButton-startIcon` span uses `display: inherit`, which resolves to `inline-flex` (inherited from the button). Per the [flex baselines](https://www.w3.org/TR/css-flexbox-1/#flex-baselines), a flex container's baseline comes from its first flex item. Since startIcon is the first flex item and is itself an inline-flex container, the baseline is recursively resolved to the SVG icon — which has no typographic baseline, so the browser synthesizes one from the icon's bottom margin edge.

With `endIcon`, the text content is the first flex item, so the correct typographic baseline is used.

### Expected behavior

The `Button` should expose its typographic text baseline regardless of whether `startIcon` is present.

**Proposed fix** (by the Opus guy, but makes sense):

Add a `::before` pseudo-element to the `Button` root that establishes a text baseline as the first flex item:

```css
.MuiButton-root::before {
  content: "\200b";
  width: 0;
  overflow: hidden;
}
```

This is invisible (zero width), doesn't affect layout (`Button` uses margins on icon slots, not gap), and works for all button variants. The `overflow: hidden` ensures the baseline is per the [CSS Align spec](https://www.w3.org/TR/css-align-3/#baseline-export). The reproduction demonstrates this fix in the second section.

### Context

We have a `SectionHeader` pattern that might have a `h2` typography on the left and buttons on the right. At some point I found out that baseline alignment looks best there. Unfortunately, the start icon breaks the alignment, as well as the loading indicator (it's been the worst, since the header was jumping when the button went loading).

I asked Claude to investigate the issue, and it didn't find a similar one. Finally we settled on this fix, and it looks general enough, so I decided to open an issue. I still have concerns that this is a bit of a weird fix, but it really makes sense in this situation. So if the team thinks it's something worth adding to the codebase, I'll be glad. If you decide it's not common enough to be added to the MUI default styles, at least this issue might help others (and their agents) to find a fix they can apply locally.

