The reason this happens is because the `package.json` in the `esm` folder (etc) is incomplete and lacks the metadata Module Federation uses to resolve package details and create the bundle.

Going back to CJS was not an option for us (breaks treeshaking and all kinds of stuff), so we ended up writing a `postinstall` script which monkeypatches the ESM modules directly in `node_modules` to make this work:

```
#!/usr/bin/env node
const fs = require('fs');
const path = require('path');

// Find all @mui packages and add version to their esm/package.json
const muiDir = path.join(__dirname, '../node_modules/@mui');

if (!fs.existsSync(muiDir)) {
  console.log('No @mui packages found, skipping patch');
  process.exit(0);
}

const packages = fs.readdirSync(muiDir);
let patched = 0;

packages.forEach(pkg => {
  const pkgPath = path.join(muiDir, pkg);
  const esmPath = path.join(pkgPath, 'esm');
  const pkgJsonPath = path.join(pkgPath, 'package.json');
  const esmPkgJsonPath = path.join(esmPath, 'package.json');

  if (fs.existsSync(esmPath) && fs.existsSync(pkgJsonPath)) {
    const pkgJson = JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync(pkgJsonPath, 'utf8'));
    const version = pkgJson.version;

    if (version) {
      fs.writeFileSync(esmPkgJsonPath, JSON.stringify({ version }, null, 2));
      console.log(`Patched @mui/${pkg}/esm/package.json with version ${version}`);
      patched++;
    }
  }
});

console.log(`Patched ${patched} @mui packages for module federation compatibility`);
```

This is of course an extremely hacky solution (if you can even call it a solution) - I am not sure whether MUI or the Module Federation team needs to look into this. It should be mentioned that we do not have this problem with ANY other pure-ESM dependency (`react-intl` etc). 

A simple fix on the MUI teams side would be to simply generate complete `package.json` files for each folder in the packages, maybe this is something worth considering @Janpot

