### Steps to reproduce

Steps:
1. Open this link to live example: [https://x38458.csb.app/](https://x38458.csb.app/)
2. Code: [https://codesandbox.io/p/sandbox/zen-waterfall-x38458](https://codesandbox.io/p/sandbox/zen-waterfall-x38458)
3. Move the slider
4. Notice both the Slider’s and the parent form’s onChange fire (check the console)
5. stopPropagation has no effect

### Current behavior

When using a Slider inside a form that has a centralized onChange handler, calling event.stopPropagation() inside the Slider’s onChange handler does not prevent the parent form’s onChange from firing.

This is a common pattern for handling form state in React: attaching a single onChange to the `form` element and letting input events bubble up. All other standard form controls (`input`, `select`, etc.) allow preventing propagation. However, MUI’s Slider does not respect this, which causes unnecessary state updates and breaks certain form patterns.

### Expected behavior

event.stopPropagation() inside Slider’s onChange should prevent the parent’s onChange from firing — similar to native input elements.

### Context

I think the problem lies in the following part of the useSlider.ts file:

```ts
const handleChange =
  onChange &&
  ((event: Event | React.SyntheticEvent, value: number | number[], thumbIndex: number) => {
    // Redefine target to allow name and value to be read.
    // This allows seamless integration with the most popular form libraries.
    // https://github.com/mui/material-ui/issues/13485#issuecomment-676048492
    // Clone the event to not override `target` of the original event.
    const nativeEvent = (event as React.SyntheticEvent).nativeEvent || event;
    // @ts-ignore The nativeEvent is function, not object
    const clonedEvent = new nativeEvent.constructor(nativeEvent.type, nativeEvent);

    Object.defineProperty(clonedEvent, 'target', {
      writable: true,
      value: { value, name },
    });

    lastChangedValue.current = value;
    onChange(clonedEvent, value, thumbIndex);
  });
```

Because clonedEvent is passed to onChange, calling e.stopPropagation() inside the user’s onChange handler does not prevent the native event from bubbling.
It gives the impression that stopPropagation isn’t working, but in reality, it’s because the original event continues to bubble.

I’d like to suggest changing this part to something like the following:

```ts
const handleChange = onChange && ((event, value, thumbIndex) => {
  const nativeEvent = (event as React.SyntheticEvent).nativeEvent || event;
  const clonedEvent = new nativeEvent.constructor(nativeEvent.type, nativeEvent);

  let propagationStopped = false;
  const originalStop = clonedEvent.stopPropagation;
  clonedEvent.stopPropagation = function () {
    propagationStopped = true;
    return originalStop.call(this);
  };

  onChange(clonedEvent, value, thumbIndex);

  if (propagationStopped && event.stopPropagation) {
    event.stopPropagation();
  }
});
```

This way, if the user calls e.stopPropagation() inside their onChange handler, the propagation will also be stopped on the original event, making the behavior consistent and more intuitive.

