```ts
const handleChange = onChange && ((event, value, thumbIndex) => {
  const nativeEvent = (event as React.SyntheticEvent).nativeEvent || event;
  const clonedEvent = new nativeEvent.constructor(nativeEvent.type, nativeEvent);

  let propagationStopped = false;
  const originalStop = clonedEvent.stopPropagation;
  clonedEvent.stopPropagation = function () {
    propagationStopped = true;
    return originalStop.call(this);
  };

  onChange(clonedEvent, value, thumbIndex);

  if (propagationStopped && event.stopPropagation) {
    event.stopPropagation();
  }
});
```

This way, if the user calls e.stopPropagation() inside their onChange handler, the propagation will also be stopped on the original event, making the behavior consistent and more intuitive.

