# Hi MUI team 👋

I’ve been experimenting with building an app that generates Material UI component boilerplate.

https://mui-code-generator.netlify.app/
_(App doesn’t work on mobile yet.)_

<img width="2672" height="1527" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/35049af7-a58c-4730-a25a-53021fe90d00" />

## The motivation:
- Writing a “proper” component in MUI (with slots, styled API, useThemeProps, useSlot, utility classes, etc.) requires quite a lot of boilerplate.
- AI tools (like ChatGPT/Claude/Gemini) can help but often hallucinate, miss details, or generate code that doesn’t compile.
- I wanted a tool that parses a small DSL/JSX-like input and generates the full component boilerplate automatically.

### Example input:
```jsx
<div.Root>
  <h1.Header>{header}</h1.Header>
  <p.Description>{description}</p.Description>
  <Button.Button>{label}</Button.Button>
  {children}
</div.Root>
```

<details>
  <summary>Click here to see generated code</summary>

```tsx
import { useThemeProps, styled } from "@mui/material";
import composeClasses from "@mui/utils/composeClasses";
import useSlot from "@mui/material/utils/useSlot";

type MyComponentProps = any;

const getMyComponentUtilityClass = (slot) => `MuiMyComponent-${slot}`;

const useUtilityClasses = (ownerState) => {
  const { classes } = ownerState;
  const slots = {
    root: ["root"],
    header: ["header"],
    description: ["description"],
    button: ["button"],
  };
  return composeClasses(slots, getMyComponentUtilityClass, classes);
};

const Root = styled("div", {
  name: "MyComponent",
  slot: "Root",
})({});

const Header = styled("h1", {
  name: "MyComponent",
  slot: "Header",
})({});

const Description = styled("p", {
  name: "MyComponent",
  slot: "Description",
})({});

const Button = styled(Button, {
  name: "MyComponent",
  slot: "Button",
})({});

const MyComponent = (inProps: MyComponentProps) => {

  const props = useThemeProps({
    props: inProps,
    name: "MyComponent",
  });

  const { children, ref, className, slots, slotProps, ...rest } = props;

  const ownerState = {};

  const classes = useUtilityClasses(ownerState);

  const externalForwardedProps = {
    slots,
    slotProps,
  } as const;

  const [RootSlot, rootSlotProps] = useSlot("root", {
    className: classes.root,
    elementType: Root,
    externalForwardedProps: {
      ...externalForwardedProps,
      ...rest,
    },
    shouldForwardComponentProp: true,
    ownerState,
    ref: ref!,
  });

  const [HeaderSlot, headerSlotProps] = useSlot("header", {
    className: classes.header,
    elementType: Header,
    externalForwardedProps: externalForwardedProps,
    ownerState,
  });

  const [DescriptionSlot, descriptionSlotProps] = useSlot("description", {
    className: classes.description,
    elementType: Description,
    externalForwardedProps: externalForwardedProps,
    ownerState,
  });

  const [ButtonSlot, buttonSlotProps] = useSlot("button", {
    className: classes.button,
    elementType: Button,
    externalForwardedProps: externalForwardedProps,
    ownerState,
  });

  return (
    <RootSlot {...rootSlotProps}>
      <HeaderSlot {...headerSlotProps}>{header}</HeaderSlot>
      <DescriptionSlot {...descriptionSlotProps}>{description}</DescriptionSlot>
      <ButtonSlot {...buttonSlotProps}>{label}</ButtonSlot>
      {children}
    </RootSlot>
  );
};

export default MyComponent;
```

</details>

#### Example generated output:
- styled slot definitions
- useThemeProps integration
- useSlot setup for each slot
- useUtilityClasses + composeClasses wiring
- A functional component ready for export

#### The app currently handles:
- Slot extraction and boilerplate generation
- Correct useSlot("slotName", {...}) usage
- Utility class functions
- Syntax highlighting in a Monaco-based playground (DSL editor on the left, generated code on the right)

#### What’s still missing / possible extensions:
- Proper TypeMap definition
- OverridableComponent and OverridableProps integration
- Automatic CreateSlotsAndSlotProps generation
- Strongly typed Props
- Extract `{variables}` and add them to the prop type as `any`. Ability to write `{something as string}` to add that to the props type section.
- Inline ability to add config to `styled()(fromInlineDSL)` from the DSL.
- [Populating the theme type](https://mui.com/material-ui/customization/creating-themed-components/#typescript)

## My question to the team & community
- Do you think there’s value in a tool like this for MUI developers?
- Would you see this as a developer-experience helper (to reduce boilerplate), or do you think it risks hiding too much of the component patterns?
- Are there best practices I should follow to make the generated output align better with the MUI codebase conventions?

## Feedback I’m looking for
- Are there important pieces of MUI’s component boilerplate that I’ve overlooked (e.g. TypeMap, ownerState typing, etc.)?  
- Would you prefer generated code to be **closer to MUI’s internal conventions** (as in the core repo), or **simplified** for app developers?  
- Are there developer-experience features you’d like to see (e.g. config options, CLI tool, VSCode plugin)?  
- Any pitfalls you think I should avoid (e.g. over-abstracting, generating code that’s hard to maintain)?

I’d love to hear your thoughts before I continue building this further.
Happy to share a demo repo if there’s interest. 🚀

Thanks!

